From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Thu, 1 Sep 1994 11:03:30 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-3] question for FAQ

At  8:15 PM 8/31/94 -0400, Elizabeth Schwartz wrote:
>If you want to put questions that don't have easy answers into the FAQ,
>some of these come up a lot:
>
>  What obligations do parents have to educate their children?
>  What obligations do children have to their parents?
>  When can a child enter into contracts?
>  Can a child be bought/sold/hired out/used as an organ donor?
>     (what about a fetus? There seem to be two Extropian positions
>      on that one)
>  Do animals have rights?
>  I believe in everything except XXX. Am I still an Extropian?

I am opposed to putting any of my views into the FAQ on topics that I feel
are appropriate for discussion on the list (those that don't have easy
answers).  I have my own opinions on these questions, but I don't want to
represent my views as "the extropian consensus" if there is significant
disagreement among extropians on that topic.  If *you* feel there is an
"easy answer" to some of these thorny questions, you're free to write up
that answer for the FAQ; but anything that requires more than a page or so
to sort out is probably an open issue and not FAQ-suitable (IMNSHO).

I answered a subset of your last question in the current FAQ (see below),
but only for "beliefs" where XXX was some particular technology.  I do
think there are some few "core" ideas to which one has to subscribe to
merit (in my view) the label "extropian":

At  6:04 PM 8/31/94 -0700, Jay Reynolds Freeman wrote:
>  I have never been able to figure out whether there is any consensus
>on this last for "XXX" == "libertarianism", or -- more provocatively
>-- for "XXX" == "that the free market is good for much".  I'm not
>seeking to reopen that one here, but it has certainly been a bone of
>sufficient contention that newcomers should be warned.
>                          --  Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

I don't consider anyone "extropian" who isn't a (small-L) libertarian;
furthermore, I apply this distinction recursively: I don't consider {anyone
who thinks that a non-libertarian can be an extropian} to be an extropian;
dot dot dot.  If you think it's OK for some criminal gang calling itself a
government to push around others who are trying to mind their own business,
then I think you're still The Enemy.  I will generate an additional FAQ
answer to cover this point.

In the FAQ, I wrote:
>Q14: If you call yourself an extropian, is it expected that you believe
>in particular technologies?
>
>A14: Emphatically not.  My definition of an extropian does not include a
>checklist of beliefs such that "So-and-So doesn't think we'll have
>self-distimming doshes by 2020; so So-and-So isn't an extropian."
>However, I do expect someone who uses the label "extropian" to, first,
>be aware of the accelerating pace of technology _in general_ and of the
>likely effects of that acceleration; and, second, be informed about and
>able to discuss intelligently most of the "core" technologies extropians
>are interested in. If Joe claims to be an extropian, it's certainly all
>right with me if he's skeptical about self-distimming doshes, and I
>eagerly want to hear his views, *if* he's reasoning from an informed
>standpoint and not from mere prejudice.  If Joe says doshes can never be
>self-distimming, but he hasn't even read John Doe's ground-breaking book
>on the subject, _Frammistats of Recursion_, then in my opinion Joe is
>just generating hot air.  Extropians are _informed_ skeptics.    [dV/dt]

Thanks for the input, all!

