My first post in a long time (I was unable to post for quite awhile...)

On Wed, 31 Aug 1994, Nick Szabo wrote:

> * is taking drugs to enhance one's motivation or intelligence
> more ethically equivalent to taking steroids or to getting
> a facelift?
> (short answer: more equivalent to getting an education.  But
> this leads to the further question, should parents or teachers
> be able to force or strongly pursuade children to take smart
> drugs?)

What is "ethically equivalent"? Personally, I have no problem with sports
people using steroids to improve performance. In fact I think they
shouldn't be barred from competition. What could be done is to have
steroid and non-steroid users competing in different leagues. In the
long term, I think this would benefit everyone, since it would spur the
creation of highly effective, safe, performance enhancers (and just
imagine all the records which would be broken..)  - Really, it's up
to the person taking them to judge their effectiveness and safety...

With regards to fact lifts (and plastic surgery in general), I regard
these on more or less the same level. Up to the person. And I think
all here would agree with me. I also feel that it is enhancing a
person, albeit only at the skin level (although it can do wonders for
self esteem as well...)

The only problem is that "society" sees both of these things as
unnatural. I suppose that's true depending on your definition of
what "natural" and "unnatural" means, although I tend to think it's
just an extension of our intelligence...

